{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"04","section":"04","chapter_title":"AI Conversations as a Teacher","title":"Classroom Communication Drafts","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting classroom communication drafts to a before-after email revision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how classroom communication drafts show a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher","cat100_supplement_classroom_communication_drafts","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/04/03"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_4_4_classroom_communication_drafts_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_4_4_classroom_communication_drafts_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_4_4_classroom_communication_drafts_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_4_4_classroom_communication_drafts_calibration"]},"content":"# Classroom Communication Drafts\n\n![Classroom Communication Drafts textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/04-04-classroom-communication-drafts.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA message to a parent has a job: it must let the reader know one concrete fact and one clear next step, without alarming them past what the situation warrants. AI tools default to vague, hedged drafts (\"there have been some issues we should discuss at some point\") because vagueness is the safe statistical average of all the emails they have seen. As of 2026 the assistants most likely to hand you such a draft are Microsoft Copilot in Outlook (\"Help me write\" / \"Help me reply\"), Gemini in Gmail, and ChatGPT, but the four-part check this section teaches is tool-agnostic and outlives model and version churn. That average is useless to a parent, who cannot act on \"some issues.\" This section teaches you to draft and check classroom messages against a fixed structure so every email carries a usable fact and a usable action.\n\nThe structure is what makes a draft checkable. Instead of asking \"does this email feel okay,\" you score it part by part: subject line, specific fact, clear action, appropriate tone. A draft that fails three of four parts is not \"almost done\"; it is unsendable, and the structure tells you exactly which part to fix.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how classroom communication drafts show a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA peer leader needs to tell a parent that three math worksheets are missing. The AI returns: \"Subject: Concern. Hi, I am reaching out because there have been some issues lately that we should probably discuss at some point when you get a chance. Thanks.\" The parent who opens this learns nothing they can act on. They do not know what is wrong, how serious it is, or what to do, and the word \"Concern\" in the subject line raises alarm without giving direction.\n\nA structured rewrite names the assignments and the dates (\"the three math worksheets due May 18, 19, and 20\"), asks for one specific action with a deadline (\"could you check her folder and reply by Friday\"), and keeps the tone calm (\"she is doing well otherwise\"). Same situation, but now the email does its job.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA classroom message is built from four parts, and each part has a pass condition you can check.\n\n| Part | Pass condition | Common failure |\n|------|----------------|----------------|\n| Subject line | names the topic in a few words | vague labels like \"Concern\" or \"Update\" |\n| Specific fact | one dated, concrete detail | \"some issues lately,\" \"not doing well\" |\n| Clear action | what the reader should do, with a deadline | \"at some point,\" \"when you can\" |\n| Tone | respectful, proportional to the situation | alarming words, blame, or false urgency |\n\nThe two parts AI fails most often are the specific fact and the clear action, because both require committing to a concrete detail the model does not actually have. The model knows the genre of a concerned email but not the dates of the missing worksheets, so it hedges. Your job is to supply the concrete values the structure demands.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A parent email reads: 'There have been some issues lately we should discuss at some point.' Which two of the four parts have failed?\" explanation=\"'Some issues lately' gives no dated, concrete detail, so the specific-fact part fails. 'At some point' gives no action with a deadline, so the clear-action part fails. These are exactly the two parts AI hedges on because it lacks the concrete values only you can supply.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Specific fact and clear action\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Subject line and tone\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Tone and clear action\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"None; the email is polite enough to send\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nMatch each part of a classroom message to its pass condition:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Subject line\",\"definition\":\"Names the topic in a few words, not a vague label like Concern\"},{\"term\":\"Specific fact\",\"definition\":\"One dated, concrete detail the reader can repeat back\"},{\"term\":\"Clear action\",\"definition\":\"What the reader should do, with a deadline\"},{\"term\":\"Tone\",\"definition\":\"Respectful and proportional to the actual stakes\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\nTone is a constraint, not a goal. The aim is not to sound warm; it is to be proportional. A missed-homework email should not read like an emergency, and an emergency should not read like a routine note. Scoring tone against the actual situation prevents both the false-alarm and the buried-lede failures.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA classroom communication drafts is a concrete document with a fixed structure: subject line, greeting, one specific fact, one clear action, and a closing. A freshman drafts a parent email, then checks it against that four-part structure. Follow the steps.\n\nStep 1. The AI returns this first draft when asked for an email about a missed assignment:\n\n```text\nSubject: Concern\n\nHi, I am reaching out because there have been some issues lately\nthat we should probably discuss at some point when you get a chance.\nThanks.\n```\n\nStep 2. Score it against the required structure. Mark each part PASS or FAIL:\n\n| Part | Requirement | Draft above |\n|------|-------------|-------------|\n| Subject line | names the topic | FAIL (\"Concern\" is vague) |\n| Specific fact | one dated, concrete detail | FAIL (\"some issues lately\") |\n| Clear action | what the parent should do | FAIL (\"at some point\") |\n| Tone | respectful, not alarming | PASS |\n\nThree of four parts fail, so the email would worry the parent without telling them anything to act on.\n\nStep 3. Rewrite each failing part with a concrete value:\n\n```text\nSubject: Math homework missing for Maria (week of May 18)\n\nHi Ms. Lopez,\nMaria has not turned in the three math worksheets due May 18, 19,\nand 20. Could you check her folder this week and reply by Friday\nso we can set a make-up plan? She is doing well otherwise.\n\nThank you,\nJ. Rivera\n```\n\nStep 4. Log the one rejected AI suggestion. The AI offered to add \"this is urgent and serious.\" You rejected it because the tone field already passed and that phrase would alarm the parent without adding a fact. The before/after plus the structure score is the evidence: a reader can see which part failed and exactly how you fixed it.\n\nScore and fix the draft one part at a time, predicting each repair before you reveal it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Score the parent email part by part\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The subject line is \\\"Concern.\\\" PASS or FAIL, and why?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"FAIL: it is a vague label that raises alarm without naming the topic\",\"PASS: any subject is fine\",\"FAIL: it is too long\",\"PASS: short subjects are always best\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"FAIL. \\\"Concern\\\" names no topic and signals alarm. Fix: \\\"Math homework missing for Maria (week of May 18).\\\"\"},{\"prompt\":\"The body says \\\"some issues lately.\\\" Which fix makes the specific-fact part pass?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Name the dated detail: \\\"the three math worksheets due May 18, 19, and 20\\\"\",\"Add the word \\\"seriously\\\"\",\"Make the sentence longer and warmer\",\"Apologize twice\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Name the dated, concrete detail. The reader can now repeat back exactly what happened, which is the fact-check standard.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The body says \\\"discuss at some point.\\\" Which fix makes the clear-action part pass?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Give an action with a deadline: \\\"check her folder and reply by Friday\\\"\",\"Say \\\"whenever you can\\\"\",\"Remove the action entirely\",\"Mark it urgent\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"An action plus a deadline. Tone already passed, so adding \\\"urgent and serious\\\" would fail the proportion check; you reject that suggestion. Three of four parts are now fixed and the email does its job.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nScore every draft against the four parts before sending, and fix by part rather than by feel. A message is ready only when the subject names the topic, the fact is dated and concrete, the action has a deadline, and the tone matches the situation.\n\nApply these three checks:\n\n1. **Fact check:** Could the reader repeat back exactly what happened, including a date or number, from your email alone?\n2. **Action check:** Does the reader know what to do and by when, or are they left to guess?\n3. **Proportion check:** Does the intensity of the wording match the actual stakes, or does it over- or under-alarm?\n\nIf the reader would have to reply \"what do you mean?\" to act, the fact or action part has failed, no matter how polished the sentences are.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is treating tone as the hardest part. AI almost always passes tone; it sounds pleasant. The parts it fails are the fact and the action, so spending your revision time softening tone while the email still says \"some issues\" fixes the wrong thing.\n\nThe second misreading is adding urgency to seem responsible. Phrases like \"this is serious and urgent\" feel diligent but usually fail the proportion check, alarming the reader without adding information. Concreteness, not intensity, is what makes a message land.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the four-part score to a positive message, since not every classroom email is bad news. You want to tell a parent their child led a group well. The AI draft says: \"Subject: Good news! Your child has been doing great and is really shining lately. Keep it up!\" Score it. Subject: PASS, it names the topic. Specific fact: FAIL, \"doing great\" names nothing concrete. Clear action: there may be no action needed, so this part can be marked N/A. Tone: PASS.\n\nThe rewrite supplies the missing fact: \"Subject: Maria led her math group today. In today's class, Maria explained how to regroup in subtraction to her three group members, and two of them solved the next problem correctly afterward.\" Now the praise is credible because it is specific. The walkthrough shows the structure is not only for bad news; the specific-fact part is what makes a compliment believable instead of generic.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: **part** on the left, **pass or fail with the reason** on the right. A strong reader does not say \"this email is weak\"; they say \"the action part fails because 'at some point' gives no deadline.\" Naming the part makes the fix obvious.\n\nA strong reader of Classroom Communication Drafts can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which of the four parts does this draft fail, and what is the exact failing phrase?\n2. What concrete value would the fact part need to pass?\n3. Does the action part give the reader a deadline, or only a vague invitation?\n4. Is the tone proportional, or does it over- or under-state the stakes?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when a reviewer can see the four-part score, the before draft, and the after draft side by side, so the fix is traceable to a failed part. Do not paraphrase the original; keep the failing draft verbatim so the improvement is visible.\n\nA useful closing note for the artifact would read: \"The first draft failed subject, fact, and action; I added the worksheet dates and a Friday deadline to fix the fact and action parts. I rejected the AI's 'this is urgent and serious' line because tone already passed and the phrase would alarm the parent without adding information.\" That ties each edit to a specific failed part and one defended rejection.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the message from a parent to a course instructor, the audience CAT 100 students email most. The four parts still apply, but the tone constraint shifts. With a parent you protect against alarm; with an instructor you protect against sounding entitled or vague. The fact part might become \"I missed the May 18 quiz because of a documented medical appointment,\" and the action part becomes a specific request (\"may I take the make-up before Friday?\") rather than an open-ended one. The structure transfers intact; what changes is which tone failure you are guarding against in the new audience.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nSee how the four-part message check connects to the chapter’s wider ideas:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher\", \"cat100_supplement_classroom_communication_drafts\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the classroom communication drafts packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/04-04-classroom-communication-drafts-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"classroom communication drafts\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"04.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the classroom communication drafts.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the classroom communication drafts for CAT 100 04.04. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to [ISTE Standards for Students 1.2 Digital Citizen](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_classroom_communication_drafts\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that your draft passes all four parts, that the fact is dated and concrete, that the action carries a deadline, and that your trace logs one rejected suggestion with a reason.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE Standards for Students 1.2 Digital Citizen (2024 edition)](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_4_classroom_communication_drafts_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_classroom_communication_drafts","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_classroom_communication_drafts","cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher"],"stem":"Which subject line passes the 'names the topic' requirement for a parent email?","options":["Subject: Concern","Subject: Math homework missing for Maria (week of May 18)","Subject: Hello","Subject: Important"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"It names the topic, the student, and the timeframe so the reader knows the content before opening.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_4_vague_subject_line","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_4_classroom_communication_drafts_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_classroom_communication_drafts","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_classroom_communication_drafts"],"stem":"The body reads: 'There have been some issues lately.' Which rewrite supplies the missing concrete fact?","options":["Things have not been going great recently.","Maria has not turned in the three math worksheets due May 18, 19, and 20.","We are a little worried about some stuff.","Please reach out when you have time."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"One dated, countable fact replaces vague worry; the reader now knows exactly what happened.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_4_no_concrete_fact","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_4_classroom_communication_drafts_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat100_supplement_classroom_communication_drafts"],"stem":"Your draft already passes the Tone check. The AI suggests adding 'this is urgent and serious.' What is the strongest move?","options":["Accept it; stronger words make the email more effective.","Reject it and log why: the Tone part already passed and the phrase alarms without adding a fact.","Put it in the subject line instead.","Add it twice for emphasis."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Rejecting against an already-passing field and recording the reason is the evidence trace.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_4_no_concrete_fact","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_4_classroom_communication_drafts_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_classroom_communication_drafts","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_classroom_communication_drafts","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Which closing line gives the reader a clear action with a deadline?","options":["We should discuss this at some point.","Could you check her folder this week and reply by Friday so we can set a make-up plan?","Let me know your thoughts whenever.","Thanks for being a great parent."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"It names a specific action (check folder, reply) and a deadline (Friday); the others leave the reader nothing to do.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_4_no_clear_action","difficulty":"medium"}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"04ac96e4d6755924a7c25286f34e521d16e172d6fd02ec92e60863f355c16992","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}